The mechanism is clear, but an observable effect requires a form to receive another submission while the relevant asynchronous action or transition is still in the state that previously triggered the failure. Whether that condition occurs depends on runtime interaction and timing.

Form transition state detection was tightened so a form is treated as already stateful only when its hook chain includes a subsequent hook. The accompanying regression test targets two closely timed submissions and expects both form actions to run without an exception.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When a form is submitted again shortly after an asynchronous submission begins, React is now intended to complete both submission attempts without throwing; the regression scenario observes two action invocations. Internally, a form with only one existing state hook is no longer considered fully upgraded to the state required for host transitions.

